From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: a733: wait for the card's busy in the host, not by CMD13

After every write request our block layer polls the card with CMD13 until it
reports READY_FOR_DATA. The vendor stack's host waits for the end of DAT0 busy
itself, so the one CMD13 the core still sends finds the card ready. This patch
makes the A733 host do the same, and offers MMC_CAP_WAIT_WHILE_BUSY with a
max_busy_timeout, which is what that wait means to the core.

THE MEASURED GAP
----------------
Raw-region comparison on one board, 2026-09-17, both kernels from a RAM root.
CMD13 requests per write request, from the mmc_request_start tracepoint:

                ours (default / performance)   vendor
    eMMC        1.1 / 1.9-2.0                  0
    SD          4.1-6.0 / 4.6-6.4              1

SD random 4k writes 684 IOPS against 953, eMMC sequential writes 137 MiB/s
against 156. The poll is block.c mmc_blk_mq_poll_completion() ->
mmc_blk_card_busy() -> __mmc_poll_for_busy(): CMD13, sleep from 32 us,
doubling. A faster CPU polls more often, which is why performance is worse.

WHAT THE A733 UM SAYS (a733-um-v0.92, SMHC0-2 register chapter)
------------------------------------------------------------------
- SMHC_THLD 0x0100, 11.3.8.29, p. 458, bit 1 BCIG (busy clear interrupt
  generation): 0 disables the busy-clear interrupt, 1 enables it; software can
  keep it off when the interrupt is not needed. Reset value
  of the register 0x0000_0005, so BCIG is 0 out of reset.
- SMHC_RINTSTS 0x0038, 11.3.8.15, p. 446, bit 13 DSE_BC "Data Start
  Error/busy clear": on a read, the host saw a bad start bit; on a write, the
  card has dropped busy after the final block. The same
  bit is DSE_BC_INT_EN in SMHC_INTMASK (11.3.8.13, p. 443) and M_DSE_BC_INT
  in SMHC_MINTSTS (11.3.8.14, p. 444).
- SMHC_STATUS 0x003C, 11.3.8.16, p. 448, bit 9 CARD_BUSY: the inverted DATA[0]
  line (1 while the card holds DAT0 busy). This is the level, and
  it is what ->card_busy already reads.
- The UM states that the SMHC1 and SMHC2 registers match those of SMHC0
  (11.3.7, p. 430). SMHC3 has a register list of its own and documents BCIG and DSE_BC as
  well. So the interrupt is not an instance or variant property on this SoC:
  SMHC0 (the SD slot) and SMHC2 (the eMMC) both have it, and those are the two
  controllers that bind sun60i_a733_mmc_cfg and sun60i_a733_emmc_cfg. The flag
  sits in those two cfgs only; the D1, A100 and H616 cfgs they fall back from
  are untouched, because their manuals are not what was read here.
- What the UM does NOT say: in which order DTC, ACD and the busy clear
  arrive, whether DTC/ACD still arrive at all once BCIG is armed, and whether
  the busy clear also covers the R1b busy of an automatic CMD12. Its own write
  procedures (11.3.6.2, 11.3.6.4, 11.3.6.6) end with a CMD13 loop. The design
  below does not depend on the order, and bounds the case where DTC/ACD never
  come.

THE CONTRACT ON THE LINUX SIDE (read in 6.18.36)
------------------------------------------------
- With MMC_CAP_WAIT_WHILE_BUSY the core skips its own poll after an R1b
  command whose busy_timeout fits max_busy_timeout: mmc_ops.c __mmc_switch()
  ("If SPI or used HW busy detection above, then we don't need to poll"),
  core.c mmc_do_erase(), mmc.c mmc_sleep(), mmc_ops.c HPI. A host that sets
  the capability must not complete such a command while the card is busy, and
  must fail it once busy_timeout has passed.
- mmc_prepare_busy_cmd() turns an R1b into a plain R1 when the timeout exceeds
  max_busy_timeout, and the core then polls as before.
- Writes are polled regardless (block.c mmc_blk_card_busy() has no capability
  test). The host changes only whether the first CMD13 finds the card ready.
  That makes the write wait an optimisation the core still checks behind us.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES
--------------------
- sunxi_mmc_set_thld() sets BCIG for a write and clears it otherwise;
  sunxi_mmc_finalize_request() clears it again with the request it was armed
  for.
- On these two controllers bit 13 counts as an error on reads only. On a write
  it is the busy clear; on a command without data it can only be a busy clear
  that latched late, and treating it as an error there would fail the CMD13
  that follows every write. Error dumps no longer print it as " SBE" on those
  requests. It is cleared (W1C) before every command and before the manual
  CMD12, for the same reason.
- A write or R1b request completes when its bus side is done (the existing
  DTC/ACD/CC condition) AND SMHC_STATUS CARD_BUSY reads idle. If DAT0 is
  already idle at that point nothing changes: the request completes in the
  hard IRQ as it always did.
- If DAT0 is busy, the request stays open. A write waits for the busy-clear
  interrupt, which only says "look now"; the request completes when that look
  finds DAT0 idle.
- A delayed work (system_long_wq) looks at DAT0 itself, with the core's
  pacing (32 us, doubling, capped at 32768 us) applied to a register read
  instead of a CMD13: after a 10 ms grace period if a write's interrupt has
  not come; at once if it came but DAT0 still reads busy (an auto-CMD12 busy
  after it, for instance), and at once for an R1b command. The grace is
  deliberate: polling at once would find DAT0 idle before the interrupt is
  serviced and the test boot could not tell whether BCIG works. If it does
  not, every busy write pays the grace, and the throughput shows it.
- Before it finalizes on the DAT0 level, the work folds SMHC_MINTSTS into the
  collected status, so an error whose interrupt is still waiting for the lock
  is reported as an error and not wiped by the RINTR clear.
- Deadline: cmd->busy_timeout for an R1b command (max_busy_timeout if the core
  left it 0), 1 s for a write. An R1b command still busy then fails with
  -ETIMEDOUT and a dev_err. A write still busy then completes without an
  error and a dev_warn, and the core's CMD13 poll takes over with its own
  bound, MMC_BLK_TIMEOUT_MS = 10 s (block.c:78). Before this patch recovery
  of a write stuck busy started after those 10 s; now after at most 11 s.
- A write whose busy clear arrives before DTC/ACD arms a 1 s bound. If the
  done bit still has not come, the request fails as a data timeout (with the
  manual stop, as any data error) and logs "busy clear without DTC/ACD" with
  the interrupt state. Without it such a request would stay open for ever,
  and the SD is root.
- Error path unchanged: any error bit finalizes at once, with no busy wait,
  and a write error still takes the manual stop.
- A write that ends busy without the interrupt having fired or being pending
  logs "write: busy ended without the busy-clear interrupt" (ratelimited,
  visible by default). That line is the direct statement that BCIG does not
  do on silicon what the UM says.
- The per-request result is a dev_dbg: "cmd N WR: busy end by busy-clear irq
  after X us" or "by DAT0 poll", marked "(busy clear before done)" when the
  interrupt came first; it also reports RINTSTS bit 18 (write busy timeout)
  if set. Enable with dyndbg="func sunxi_mmc_end_busy_wait +p".
- A write that ends busy by the poll, after the full grace, with bit 13
  neither handled nor pending logs the warning above; an earlier poll (a work
  already queued for a previous wait) proves nothing and stays silent.
- At request start the driver clears a stale bit 13 (W1C) on these two
  controllers, so a busy clear that latched after the previous write was
  finalized cannot reach a following read as a start-bit error.
- max_busy_timeout = 60000 ms. The wait is timed in software, so no register
  sets a ceiling. The value decides which R1b commands the core hands to the
  host and how large a discard mmc_do_calc_max_discard() builds. 60 s is the
  core's MMC_ERASE_TIMEOUT_MS, which that sizing already used while
  max_busy_timeout was 0, so the discard geometry does not change.

DELIBERATELY NOT USED
---------------------
- SMHC_CMD bit 17 R1B_BUSY_HW_DETECT with bit 16 R1B_RESP (11.3.8.7, p. 440)
  and the SMHC_RINTSTS bit 17 R1B_BUSY_CLR interrupt (signals the end
  of an R1b busy, or that there was none). This would be the UM's own path for R1b commands.
  It is left out of this patch: it changes how the controller runs the
  command itself, the vendor uses it only when it starts command queueing
  (sunxi-mmc.c:3476), and R1b commands are rare on the measured path (1-2
  CMD6 per trace phase, the cache flush). One new hardware behaviour per
  board cycle. The DAT0 level is enough for the capability's contract.
- SMHC_RINTSTS bit 18 DATA_WR_BUSY_TO_INT with SMHC_WR_BUSY_TMOUT 0x015C
  (11.3.8.40, reset 0x00FFFFFF, the limit is WR_BUSY_TO_LMT plus one). The UM
  gives no unit. A hardware timeout of unknown length could end a legitimate
  busy early, so the deadline stays in software and the interrupt stays
  masked. Masking does not settle whether that counter runs out while BCIG is
  armed and does something else (the vendor never programs it either): that
  is unmeasured, and the dev_dbg above reads the raw bit so the test boot can
  answer it.

THE VENDOR, READ AS A HINT (radxa/allwinner-bsp cubie-aiot-v1.4.8)
------------------------------------------------------------------
The register semantics above come from the UM. The vendor source confirms the
bit use (sunxi-mmc.c:2741-2742 and 1189-1190 set and clear BCIG through
sunxi_mmc_hw_wbusy_wait_v4p5x(), sunxi-mmc-v4p5x.c:1098;
sunxi-mmc.c:1066 drops bit 13 from its error flags for those writes). Its
measured 1 CMD13 per SD write shows that its host waits out the busy, not that
the interrupt does it: the vendor also re-checks DAT0 after the interrupt and
polls it in a thread when still busy (sunxi-mmc.c:1125-1165, 1548-1581), and
the trace cannot tell the two apart. Where it differs, and what this patch
does instead:

1. It completes a write on the busy clear alone. For writes it removes
   DATA_OVER and AUTO_COMMAND_DONE from the interrupt mask
   (sunxi-mmc.c:2688-2699), so the request is finalized on bit 13 without
   DTC or ACD ever being required. We keep the done bits and require them and
   an idle DAT0.
2. Nothing bounds a busy clear that never comes except its generic 5 s
   transfer watchdog (SUNXI_TRANS_TIMEOUT, sunxi-mmc.c:87, 270-345), whose
   recovery path gates the bus clock and resets the host. We fall back to
   looking at DAT0 after 10 ms and say in the log that the interrupt was
   missing.
3. It ignores the command's busy_timeout. sunxi_check_r1_ready_may_sleep()
   waits max_busy_timeout - 20 ms whatever the request asked for, and says so
   ("we don't use the timeout value in mrq_busy->busy_timeout",
   sunxi-mmc.c:1560-1566). With its max_busy_timeout of 600000 ms
   (SUNXI_DEF_MAX_R1B_TIMEOUT_MS, sunxi-mmc.c:85, 4313) a CMD6 whose card
   gives 250 ms can hold the host ten minutes before it fails. We honour
   cmd->busy_timeout, as the capability requires.
4. Its first 10 ms of that wait are a busy loop with cond_resched()
   (sunxi-mmc.c:1489-1494, "dead wait"). We sleep with the core's pacing.
5. Its 600 s max_busy_timeout also lets the core build discards sized for a
   ten-minute erase (mmc_do_calc_max_discard). Not adopted; see 60 s above.

VERIFIED / NOT VERIFIED
-----------------------
Read by an independent second reviewer before the build; its findings are
folded in. Applies clean at --fuzz=0 on the full series. NOT built locally. This
header was written before the board run; the 2026-09-17 board run of this patch
is summarised in `mmc-sunxi-a733-emmc-host-software-queue` (THE MEASURED GAP).

The test plan, as concrete observables on the test kernel (localversion -mmcbusy),
same method as 2026-09-17 (raw-region benchmark on our
kernel, RAM root, same benchmark binary):
 1. In every write phase of the trace, on both media and both governors:
    count(cmd_opcode=13) <= count(cmd_opcode=25) + count(cmd_opcode=6).
    (One CMD13 per write from mmc_blk_card_busy, one per cache-flush CMD6
    from mmc_switch_status. The CMD6 costs the same one CMD13 before and after
    this patch: its busy poll used ->card_busy already, mmc_ops.c:475.)
    Before: SD 1640-4918 CMD13 against 400-768 CMD25; eMMC 451-1571 CMD13
    against 402-769 CMD25+CMD6.
 2. SD random 4k write rises from ~684 IOPS towards ~953 (vendor); eMMC
    sequential write from ~137 MiB/s towards ~156.
 3. Zero "busy ended without the busy-clear interrupt", zero "card still
    busy", zero "busy clear without DTC/ACD", zero "error -110" on a status
    or write, zero "data error, sending stop command", in journalctl -k of the
    whole session. A single missing-interrupt line refutes the BCIG reading;
    dyndbg on sunxi_mmc_end_busy_wait then shows which path ended each wait.
 4. Unchanged: eMMC enumerates HS400 and SD SDR104 on a cold boot, all
    raw-region verify_bad=0, read numbers within the 2 % spread.

Assisted-by: Claude
